config MTK_FLASHLIGHT_DEBUG
	bool "Mediatek flashlight debugging messages"
	depends on MTK_FLASHLIGHT
	default n
	help
	  Say Y here if you want the flashlight to produce a bunch of debug
	  messages to the system log.

	  Select this if you are having a problem with flashlight support
	  and want to see more of what is going on.

config MTK_FLASHLIGHT_PT
	bool "Mediatek flashlight power throttling"
	depends on MTK_FLASHLIGHT
	default n
	help
	  This is for flashlight power throttling.

	  Power throttling will limit flashlight function,
	  such as disable flashlight, decrease brightness level or
	  change flash mode to torch mode.

config MTK_FLASHLIGHT_PT_STRICT
    bool "Mediatek flashlight power throttling (strict)"
	depends on MTK_FLASHLIGHT_PT
	default n
	help
	  This is for flashlight strict power throttling.

	  Strict power throttling will disable flashlight when
	  over-current, low battary voltage and low battary percentage
	  situations.

config MTK_FLASHLIGHT_DLPT
    bool "Mediatek flashlight dynamic low power throttling"
	depends on MTK_FLASHLIGHT
	default n
	help
	  This is for flashlight dynamic low power throttling.

	  Dynamic low power throttling is a mechanism to monitor
	  the current of battary dynamicly. It will start by request
	  and notice user by kernel notifier.

#
# THE FACTORY OPTIONS THAT WERE MISSING, declared on 2026-08-26.
#
# The factory .config has them all at =y and our tree did not declare them
# at all: `olddefconfig` discards a symbol no Kconfig defines, so there was
# no way to turn them on.
#
# BEWARE, and this has to be said before reading them as "added features":
# **none of these guards protects code that exists in this tree.**
# Turning them on aligns the .config with the factory kernel and does not change
# a byte of the binary. They serve two purposes:
#
#   1. removing fifteen differences from the config comparison, which is one
#      of the three measurements of the project;
#   2. giving the code somewhere to attach to when it gets written.
#
# Where they sit is our own choice, by subsystem: the factory Kconfig is not
# among the recovered files, and deriving it from the binary is impossible
# -- a Kconfig symbol leaves no trace in compiled code.
#

config WTK_MAIN_FLASHLIGHT_CH0
	bool "main flash channel 0 (factory name)"
	default y
	help
	  The factory .config has CONFIG_WTK_MAIN_FLASHLIGHT_CH0=y.

	  In this tree the name appears in four files -- flashlight-device.c,
	  flashlights-mt6370.c and mt6370_pmu_charger.c -- but ONLY IN COMMENTS:
	  no #ifdef tests it. The flashlight block measures 90 functions out of
	  90 equal to the factory ones all the same, so nothing has been missing
	  so far, and turning this entry on does not change the generated code.
